{"command": "cd /testbed && cat .specify/memory/constitution.md", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "# cachetools Constitution\n\n## Core Principles\n\n### I. Preserve Existing Public API Behavior\nAll existing cachetools public APIs MUST remain functional and backward-compatible. New components (e.g., TaggedCache) MUST NOT alter the behavior, signatures, or return types of existing classes and functions. When extending the package, additions are additive only — no existing import paths, method signatures, or documented behaviors may change.\n\nRationale: cachetools is a widely used library; breaking changes erode trust and force costly migrations.\n\n### II. Attach Testable Acceptance Criteria to Requirements\nEvery requirement MUST have explicit, testable acceptance criteria that can be verified by automated tests. Requirements without testable criteria are incomplete. Tests MUST cover both happy paths and edge cases (invalid inputs, boundary conditions, error conditions).\n\nRationale: testability ensures requirements are unambiguous and verifiable.\n\n### III. Distinguish Observed Evidence from Unverified Assertions\nClaims about behavior, performance, or correctness MUST be supported by observable evidence (test results, benchmarks, logs). Unverified assumptions MUST be documented separately and flagged for validation. Code comments and documentation MUST not present assumptions as facts.\n\nRationale: evidence-based development prevents hidden defects and enables confident refactoring.\n\n### IV. Prefer Simple Scoped Implementations\nImplementations MUST be narrowly scoped to the problem at hand. Avoid over-engineering, unnecessary abstractions, or features not explicitly required. When a simple solution suffices, use it. Complexity MUST be justified and documented.\n\nRationale: simple code is easier to test, review, and maintain.\n\n### V. Document Unresolved Assumptions and Regressions\nAll unresolved assumptions, known limitations, and potential regressions MUST be recorded in the project's documentation (e.g., HANDOFF.md, inline comments, or issue trackers). Assumptions MUST include what was assumed, why, and what would invalidate them. Regressions MUST describe the affected behavior and the path to resolution.\n\nRationale: transparency about unknowns prevents silent failures and accelerates future work.\n\n## Additional Constraints\n\nTaggedCache (src/cachetools/tagged.py) MUST use cachetools.LRUCache as its bounded storage backend. All cachetools public APIs MUST continue to function unchanged. TaggedCache values are deep-copied on both input (put) and output (get). Tags are validated strictly: they must be a finite iterable of strings, a bare string must be rejected, and non-string entries must raise ValueError before any state mutation.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n1. Write tests before implementation (red-green-refactor cycle).\n2. Verify all upstream tests pass before and after changes.\n3. Run acceptance_public.py tests as the public acceptance gate.\n4. Document any unresolved assumptions in HANDOFF.md.\n5. Keep changes minimal and scoped; justify any complexity.\n\n## Governance\n\nThis constitution supersedes all other development practices for the cachetools project. Amendments require:\n- A documented change description.\n- Version bump per semantic versioning (MAJOR for principle removals/redefinitions, MINOR for new sections, PATCH for clarifications).\n- Updated ratification metadata.\n\nAll PRs and reviews MUST verify compliance with these principles. Complexity MUST be justified.\n\n**Version**: 1.0.0 | **Ratified**: 2026-09-18 | **Last Amended**: 2026-09-18\n"}